http://opendata.inra.fr/AHOL/AHOL_0005001,disease,disease,BioPortal: AHOL; BioPortal: LHO,include,,,curated_ontology,native,unclear_model,"The definition reads like three different definitions copy pasted. ""Damage on health"" is very broad and encompasses entities like suffocation which is clearly not a disease. ""The modifications of an organism defending itself against an infection or other damager (!) factor"" does exclude a lot of entities typically denoted as diseases. There could be a potential malignancy against which is no observable sign of defence. ""Perturbation of the animal's normal function"" is again very broad. Holding my breath can be argued as a perturbation of normal function.",poor,absent,poor,good,1,,MM,2026-07-04,,,,,Included with the caveat of the animal only scope of the ontology.
http://www.projecthalo.com/aura#Disease,disease,Disease,BioPortal: AURA,include,,,curated_ontology,native,unclear_model,Very broad definition that could be: the abnormal condition of low oxygen because of very high altitude during skydiving causing hypoxemia. Low oxygen clearly isn't a disease. The class itself isn't modelled very ontologically. Subclass of aura:Disease is aura:Genetic_disorder next to aura:Cancer. aura:Genetic_disorder has as subclass aura:Gap_in_segmentation which is a mutation in common sense. Its nephew aura:Huntington's-Disease is a disease in common sense. The parent aura:Intangible-Entity would need further explanation. Because aura:Cancer which is a aura:Disease and thus a aura:IntangibleEntity is very tangible in common understanding.,poor,absent,poor,good,1,,MM,2026-07-04,,,,,
http://www.bioassayontology.org/bao#BAO_0002927,disease,disease,BioPortal: BAO,include,,,curated_ontology,native,unclear_model,"Again, a pragmatic rather than ontological approach to disease. No definition, no comment, its only superclass bao:assay biology component is hardly understandable. Probably very scope specific.",absent,absent,poor,good,1,,MM,2026-07-04,,,,,
